        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I wanted to do the equivalent of `:vsplit <filename>` where the filename is taken from the currently selected file in the mini.files popup. Is there no way to directly open the file under cursor in the mini.files popup to open in the split?\r\n\r\nYes, there is. It requires modifying the example code a little bit. There is a line there which sets target window and you'd want to immediately follow up with opening file. There is an exported function in 'mini.files' for that: [`go_in()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/a683bfe8e03293e3bb079e24c94e51977756a3ec/doc/mini-files.txt#L693). This will be an addition of a single line.\r\n(I'd like to make it clear that this answer is an attempt at \"instead of giving a fish, teach a person to fish, and they'll never be hungry\")\r\n\r\nNote: if you are using 'mini.operators', beware that typing `gs` for a horizontal will be followed with a delay because there is a `gss` mapping coming from it (so Neovim wait for `'timeoutlen'` milliseconds for extra keys).",
            "created_at": "2024-03-23T09:05:03Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/760#issuecomment-2016419035",
            "id": 2016419035,
            "issue_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/760",
